steps.body:
An `if` runs its block only when the condition in parentheses is true. Otherwise the block is skipped.

```cpp
if (temperature > 30) {
    std::cout << "Hot day\n";
}
```

The comparison operators are `==`, `!=`, `<`, `>`, `<=` and `>=`. Each one produces a `bool`, so you can store it (`const bool hot = temperature > 30;`) or print it. Separate `if` statements are independent: each checks its own condition.

Watch the `=` vs `==` trap. `if (x = 5)` compiles, assigns 5 to `x`, and is always true. Always write braces, even around one line. Without them only the next statement belongs to the `if`, so a line you add later would run every time.

To print a comparison, wrap it in parentheses. `<<` binds tighter than `!=`, so `std::cout << score != 100;` fails with `error: no match for 'operator!=' (operand types are 'std::basic_ostream<char>' and 'int')`. Write `std::cout << (score != 100)` instead.

Two traps to know: `==` on doubles is fragile (`0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3` is false), and `-1 < 1u` is false because `-1` converts to a huge unsigned value.

example.code:
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    const int temperature = 31;
    if (temperature > 30) {
        std::cout << "Hot day\n";
    }
    if (temperature <= 0) {
        std::cout << "Freezing\n";
    }
    std::cout << std::boolalpha;
    std::cout << (temperature == 31) << '\n';
    std::cout << (temperature < 0) << '\n';
    return 0;
}

