practice.solution:
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    const int score = 72;
    if (score >= 60) {
        std::cout << "Passed\n";
    }
    if (score == 100) {
        std::cout << "Perfect\n";
    }
    std::cout << std::boolalpha;
    std::cout << (score != 100) << '\n';
    std::cout << (score > 90) << '\n';
    return 0;
}

steps.body:
Combine conditions with the logical operators. `&&` (and) is true when both sides are true. `||` (or) is true when at least one side is. `!` (not) flips a `bool`.

```cpp
if (age >= 12 && height >= 140) { ... }
if (hasPass || age < 5) { ... }
if (!hasPass) { ... }
```

Both operators **short-circuit**: `&&` skips its right side when the left is false, and `||` skips it when the left is true. That makes guards safe, because the risky part never runs:

```cpp
if (count != 0 && total / count > 5) { ... }
```

When `count` is 0, the division is never evaluated, so there is no division by zero.

`!` binds tightest, even tighter than comparisons, so write `!(age > 18)`, not `!age > 18`. Then comes `&&`, then `||`. So `isVip || age >= 18 && hasTicket` means `isVip || (age >= 18 && hasTicket)`. When you mix `&&` and `||`, add parentheses to show the grouping you mean: `(isVip || age >= 18) && hasTicket` is a different rule.

example.code:
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    const int age = 14;
    const int height = 150;
    const bool hasPass = false;
    if (age >= 12 && height >= 140) {
        std::cout << "Can ride\n";
    }
    if (hasPass || age < 5) {
        std::cout << "Free entry\n";
    }
    if (!hasPass) {
        std::cout << "Buy a ticket\n";
    }
    return 0;
}

